{"id": "sevt_01THje9yya8SmahDe6hbKjDK", "content": [{"text": "The checkout p99 spike is caused by deploy `ccf7a0f`, which turned a single batched `order_items` query into one query per open order.\n\n**Timeline (UTC, 2026-04-22)**\n- **14:46:05**: checkout deploy `ccf7a0f` (frank, \"refactor order summary builder for readability\").\n- **14:46**: checkout p99 goes from 70 ms to 3006 ms. It stays between 3.2 s and 4.3 s through 14:59.\n- **14:46:08**: first per-row `order_items` query in the log. Before 14:46 there are only `IN (...)` batch queries (for example 14:00:23, 8 rows, 6 ms).\n- **14:55**: error rate goes from 0% to roughly 0.25\u20130.33%, and checkout ERROR/WARN lines start to appear around the same time.\n- Per-minute per-row query volume after the deploy is about 40\u2013160 per minute. The minute counts are rough, since I counted log lines and did not isolate one request.\n- Other recent deploys don't line up: inventory at 12:20 and cart at 14:18, both well before the spike.\n\n**Diff**\nThe old code ran one `OrderItem.objects.filter(order_id__in=ids)` and grouped in memory. The new code runs `OrderItem.objects.filter(order_id=order.order_id)` inside a loop over the open orders. The commit also adds a `log.debug` line on each iteration. Latency therefore scales with the number of open orders per user, and each lookup adds a DB round trip.\n\n**Confidence:** High on the mechanism. The p99 step, deploy timing, and query-pattern change all line up. I did not check the DB-side metrics (`db_pool_utilization`), because the deploy correlation was already conclusive, so I can't say how much of the latency is pool contention versus round-trip cost. The error-rate increase starting at 14:55 is small and I haven't tied it to a specific cause.\n\n**Fix:** Roll back checkout to the commit before `ccf7a0f`, or restore the batched `order_id__in` query and in-memory grouping. The rollback is the faster option for mitigation.\n\n**Root cause:** `ccf7a0f` \u2014 the readability refactor replaced the batched `order_items` query with one query per open order, so checkout's latency grows with each order and p99 jumped to about 3\u20134 s after the 14:46 deploy.", "type": "text"}], "processed_at": "2026-10-14T12:03:00.777354Z", "type": "agent.message"}
